{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305212,"nonce":"mU6k6N49r6BhnrXMrrq1S2Ai8v8=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiJxw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/29","origin":"github"},"title":"Upstream project is unlicensed","message":"`vbuell/torram` carries no licence file, so the original code has no grant of rights beyond what GitHub's Terms of Service allow: viewing and forking **within GitHub**. Redistribution elsewhere, including publishing to PyPI, is not covered.\n\nThis fork adds Apache-2.0, which cleanly covers contributions made here, but no downstream file can retroactively license someone else's work. A `NOTICE` file credits the original project.\n\nOptions, roughly in order of how cleanly they resolve it:\n\n1. Ask vbuell to add a licence to the original repository. Cheapest fix if they respond.\n2. Distribute from git only, which stays inside the fork right.\n3. Rewrite the core from a specification rather than from the original source. The architectural rewrite would produce code owned outright here, resolving this as a side effect.\n\nWorth weighing when prioritising the rewrite.","files":null}]}